</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT have emerged as a transformative force in natural language processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Their capabilities in text generation, question-answering, and language translation have reached unprecedented levels, propelling significant advancements in artificial intelligence research.
More recently, LLMs have extended their reach through the integration of multi-modal capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
Audio LLMs, such as those powering voice assistants like GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, have become a focal research point <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.
These speech models can process voice input and generate voice output directly, enabling more intuitive, adaptable, and high-quality speech interactions that outperform traditional text-based systems.
This progress portends substantial improvements in user experiences across various applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">High-quality benchmarks are usually essential for thoroughly understanding the performance of LLMs and targeting areas for enhancement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
However, most speech LLMs evaluation benchmarks remain proprietary, and there are only a few public benchmarks that are compatible with recent speech LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
Nonetheless, they have several notable limitations that hinder an accurate and comprehensive evaluation of models in real-world speech interactions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Biased query source</span>. Current benchmarks usually directly reuse queries from text-based datasets without thorough filtering and examination, which leads to mismatches with actual application scenarios of speech LLMs.
These queries may lack the diversity and specificity required for spoken interactions, which limits the coverage of various real-world use cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absence of acoustic features</span>. Most evaluation sets are synthesized using high-quality text-to-speech (TTS) models, resulting in clean and homogeneous audio data.
In real-world applications, speech LLMs must handle complex acoustic conditions, including various types of noise (e.g., background human speech, environmental sounds) and diverse speaker attributes such as age, gender, and timbre.
The lack of such characteristics limits the assessment of model robustness under realistic conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Untargeted evaluation method</span>. Existing benchmarks and evaluation protocols are often not tailored to the unique characteristics of speech scenarios. For example, evaluation prompts are rarely designed to capture speech-specific phenomena such as the impact of tone, pauses, homophones, or stuttering on semantic interpretation. Moreover, widely used benchmarks like VoiceBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> primarily measure output in the text modality, rather than directly evaluating the quality of generated speech. Additionally, the evaluation criteria in speech scenarios differ from those in text. For instance, while text benchmarks may emphasize long-form content, speech users typically prioritize efficient information acquisition, and excessively long utterances can be impractical.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present WildSpeech-Bench, a holistic benchmark specifically tailored to end-to-end speech LLMs evaluation.
As shown in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, it has notable advantages in both dataset construction and evaluation.
To construct this benchmark, we first systematically curated real-world text-based chat data, then filtered for utterances commonly encountered in spoken scenarios and categorized the data accordingly.
In speech data synthesis, we introduced controlled variations in speaker attributes such as age, gender, and timbre to better reflect the diversity of real users. Furthermore, to simulate realistic acoustic environments, we augmented the query audio with two types of noise: (1) background human speech, generated by mixing in random speech samples to mimic conversational noise, and (2) a range of non-speech noises, including animal sounds, natural sounds (e.g., wind, rain), and human sounds (e.g., coughing, laughter, footsteps). This diverse noise augmentation enables a thorough evaluation of the system’s robustness to various acoustic interferences.
In addition, we enriched the dataset with speech-specific challenges, such as queries whose meanings change due to differences in pauses or emotions, sentences containing near-homophones, and utterances with difficult-to-recognize stuttering.
To further improve evaluation accuracy, we designed customized checklists for suitable queries and developed evaluation criteria prompts tailored to the speech scenario.
We conducted extensive experiments and analysis on our benchmark.
The results demonstrated that open-source models have notable gaps with proprietary models like GPT-4o and all tested models have much room for improvement, especially when handling speech-specific challenges.
Our benchmark is expected to provide novel insights for the future development and optimization of speech LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To sum up, our major contributions include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Realistic and diverse data curation:</span> We curate real-world chat queries relevant to speech scenarios and synthesize speech data with controlled variations in age, gender, timbre and voice distance, ensuring data diversity, complexity and coverage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Audio-specific challenge simulation:</span> We introduce speech-specific challenges to the dataset, including queries with semantic ambiguity due to pauses or emotions, sentences containing near-homophones, and utterances with stuttering. We also explore the influence of different background noise to better evaluate model robustness in realistic conditions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-grained performance evaluation:</span> We design tailored checklists and evaluation prompts for suitable queries, providing fine-grained and scenario-specific assessment criteria that better match human requirements and preferences in speech conversations.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
