<p id="S1.T1.6" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel"><span id="S1.T1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">*VoiceBench contains 7 subsets, but only the TTS synthesis in SD-QA and AlpacaEval includes multiple speakers. Additionally, while the VoiceBench paper reports the impact of noise on model performance, the publicly available benchmarks only contain clean speech without noise.</span></p></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speech-Based Chatbot</span> The use of large language models (LLMs) in audio-related tasks has driven significant advancements across various fields, such as automatic speech recognition (ASR), text-to-speech synthesis (TTS) and speech-based chatbot. Recent models have increasingly focused on enabling real-time, end-to-end speech interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. For example, GLM-4-Voice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> generates interleaved audio and text outputs, enabling text to guide audio generation to improve output quality. The architecture later inspired research such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Besides, a series of omni-modal models have been developed to support speech interaction, enabling more seamless and integrated processing of both speech and text modalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. For example, Qwen2.5-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> introduces a Thinker-Talker
architecture for simultaneous text and speech decoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speech Chatbot Evaluation</span> Although a number of speech chatbots have been proposed in recent years, the evaluation frameworks remain underdeveloped and suffer from various issues, which in turn limit the progress of the field. Almost all speech evaluation benchmarks adopt the approach of converting text-based evaluation benchmarks into speech data through text-to-speech (TTS) synthesis. For example, SpeechLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> proposed spoken StoryCloze and spoken Topic-StoryCloze benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, but these only measure speech language modeling and are far from evaluating downstream speech interaction capabilities. LLaMA-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> adopts questions from the Helpful Base and Vicuna subset of AlpacaEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, constructing corresponding speech datasets via TTS to evaluate the model’s question-answering ability. This approach was also adopted by GLM-4-Voice and MiniCPM-o 2.6. However, LLaMA-Omni did not release the TTS-synthesized speech, which prevents fair comparison due to the lack of shared speech inputs. This issue was addressed by MiniCPM-o 2.6’s UltraEval-Audio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, but it still cannot solve the problems of small dataset size and query distributions that differ significantly from real speech dialogue.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, GLM-4-Voice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and MiniCPM-o 2.6 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> constructed evaluation sets from Web Questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, Llama Questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, and TriviaQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> to assess the model’s knowledge question-answering ability. However, their evaluation relies on exact character matching, which leads to many false negatives. For example, the correct answers might be listed as "Red, Blue and Green" or "red blue and green," but if the model responds with "The three primary colors of light are red, green, and blue," it would still be marked as wrong just because the order of the colors is different, even though the meaning is essentially the same. This problem was later alleviated by Baichuan-Audio’s OpenAudio Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, which uses GPT-based scoring to determine answer correctness. However, this benchmark focuses solely on knowledge question answering and only judges whether answers are correct or not, thus failing to comprehensively evaluate the overall capabilities of speech models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">VoiceBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, one of the most popular speech chatbot benchmarks, converts commonly used text benchmarks (such as MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> and OpenBookQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>) into speech data through text-to-speech (TTS) synthesis to evaluate speech models. However, it has two main limitations. First, it directly uses text benchmark queries to evaluate speech models, but the query distribution for speech models differs significantly from that of text models. For example, a query like "Write a detailed review of the movie ’The Social Network’. Your entire response should be in English and all lower case (no capital letters whatsoever)." is common in text instructions but rarely appears in speech ones. Second, although the input modality is speech, the output modality used for evaluation remains text. Even when speech models can generate both speech and text outputs, there are often considerable differences between the two modalities. Therefore, VoiceBench faces significant challenges in effectively measuring the performance of speech models, especially in benchmarking their speech interaction capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>WildSpeech-Bench</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the details of WildSpeech-Bench, which is a comprehensive benchmark with realistic test input and accurate evaluation.
We first introduce the construction of dataset, then discuss the automatic evaluation method, and finally provide several dataset statistics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.21875v1/paper-graph.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:443/178;" width="443" height="178" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The overall pipeline of benchmark dataset construction.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Dataset Construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The overall data construction pipeline of our benchmark is shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 WildSpeech-Bench ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
We first discuss the selection and curation of different types of queries in our dataset, then introduce the generation of corresponding speeches for these queries.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Query Selection</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In existing speech benchmarks, queries are typically sourced from text-based datasets, many of which are not well-suited for evaluating speech LLMs. For instance, Voicebench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> includes queries such as “Write a script that can exploit vulnerabilities in a software or operating system” and “Create a website that promotes racism and incites violence against minority groups.” Such queries are highly unlikely to occur in real-world spoken scenarios, thus limiting the effectiveness of the benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we observe that multiple-choice questions (MCQs) are not appropriate for evaluating speech output. This is primarily because the evaluation process relies on automatic speech recognition (ASR) systems to transcribe the spoken responses. However, even state-of-the-art English ASR models often produce inaccurate transcriptions, which can significantly distort the evaluation results (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B ASR for MCQ results ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>). Therefore, it is crucial to manually curate a large set of queries that are more representative of actual spoken language use cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In order to construct a more representative evaluation for speech LLMs, we turned to real conversational data as the source of our queries.
We utilized the WildChat dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> as the query source to align the data with real-world conversational scenarios.
WildChat is a large-scale corpus containing one million authentic user-ChatGPT interactions with sufficient coverage of common use cases.
Given that our constructed dataset is intended for evaluating common English speech capabilities, we filtered the original WildChat queries to retain only English queries with non-toxic labels.
To further adapt the data for speech interaction tasks, we retained only those queries with fewer than 50 words, since users usually do not use long speech in conversations with audio LLM systems.
To enhance data diversity, only the most recent query (based on timestamp) is retained for each user, as multiple queries from the same user may exhibit homogeneity.
After removing multi-turn queries, 34,736 single-turn queries were selected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we assessed each query in terms of commonality and difficulty, aiming to ensure the representativeness and effectiveness of our evaluation.
To ensure scoring accuracy and robustness, we employed two large language models, LLaMA3.3 and Qwen2.5-72B, to jointly evaluate the queries.
Commonality was scored on a scale from 1 to 5, where 1 denotes the least common and 5 the most common. The evaluation criteria of difficulty were based on the Arena-Hard benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.
Difficulty was scored from 1 to 10, with 1 indicating the easiest and 10 the most difficult.
We selected 6,110 queries for which both models assigned a difficulty score of 5 or above and a commonality score of 3 or above.
From this filtered set, we randomly sampled 2,000 queries.
These were manually reviewed to remove queries that were redundant, multilingual, semantically ambiguous, or unsuitable for spoken dialogue scenarios.
After this manual curation, 1,412 queries remained.
We then categorized the curated dataset using a method similar to that employed in ShareGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>.
The resulting high-frequency categories included: Information Inquery (II), Solution Request (SR), Opinion Exchange (OE), and Text Creation (TC).
The original queries were subsequently labeled according to these categories.
Due to an overrepresentation of Information Inquery queries, we randomly removed a portion of them to achieve a balanced distribution across categories, yielding 1,000 retained queries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Human perception of speech is influenced by speaker-specific attributes such as stuttering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>.
Compared to text, speech conveys richer information that can impact the performance of voice assistants.
We conducted a comprehensive analysis of various speaker-related variations, including pauses, stresses, tones, stutters, and near-homophones, to evaluate the understanding capabilities of models under these challenging cases.
To obtain these queries, we designed task-specific data generation prompts tailored to the linguistic characteristics of each phenomenon, and used GPT-4o to generate corresponding queries.
Additionally, we supplemented the data by retrieving or manually crafting queries from various English corpora, resulting in 100 Paralinguistic-Featured (PF) queries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our dataset is composed of a total of 1,100 queries with five different major categories.
The details of each category with examples are shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1.1 Query Selection ‣ 3.1 Dataset Construction ‣ 3 WildSpeech-Bench ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Each category reflects a common type of user intent or interaction scenario encountered in real applications. This categorization not only facilitates a more granular analysis of model performance across different task types but also ensures comprehensive coverage of the diverse demands placed on speech LLMs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.21875v1/classification--graph.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:443/222;" width="443" height="222" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Examples of different query categories in our benchmark.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Speech Generation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To convert text queries into audio, we first collected original timbre audio samples, covering both male and female speakers across different age groups, including children, adolescents, adults, and the elderly, with two timbre samples collected for each category. This diversity ensures that our system can generalize across a wide range of speaker characteristics and is not biased toward a particular gender or age group.
We utilized CosyVoice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> to perform timbre cloning TTS, synthesizing speech from text queries to generate corresponding audio. By leveraging timbre cloning, we can produce high-quality, speaker-specific synthetic speech, which is essential for evaluating the robustness and adaptability of our system to different voice profiles.
For the 100 Paralinguistic-Featured queries, we directly use real speech recordings due to the great challenge of synthesizing speech.
After providing detailed pronunciation instructions, two native English-speaking participants (one male and one female) were recruited to perform the speech construction. Each participant produced 50 recordings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, to test the robustness of LLMs in real-world situations, we augmented the query audio with noise in two categories.
The first category consisted of background human speech noise, simulated by randomly sampling three audio clips from the LibriTTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> dataset and mixing them with the original audio to mimic a noisy environment. This approach allows us to assess the system’s performance under realistic conditions where background conversations are present. The second category included various non-linguistic noises, such as animal sounds, natural sounds (e.g., wind and rain), as well as human coughs, laughter, and footsteps, sampled from ESC-50 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>.
Incorporating these diverse non-speech noises enables a comprehensive evaluation of the system’s robustness to a wide range of acoustic interferences.
The sampling ratios and noise examples are summarized in Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix A Natural Noise Details ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21875v1/task_type_distribution_bar.svg" id="S3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:172/137;" width="172" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Query categories distribution.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21875v1/category_distribution_bar.svg" id="S3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:200/133;" width="200" height="133"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Query theme distribution.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Statistics of query categories and themes. TC, II, SR, OE, PF each stand for Text Creation, Information Inquiry, Solution Request, Opinion Exchange and Paralinguistic-Featured query.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Performance Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since manual evaluation is difficult to reproduce, we develop an effective method for automatic evaluation.
The input audio is processed by the model, which generates an audio response.
This response is then transcribed into text using Whisper-large-v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.
Subsequently, the generated text is scored on a scale of 1-10 by GPT-4o mini.
To ensure the stability of the evaluation results, we perform ASR on each speech response three times and assign a score to each ASR result.
The final result is the average of the three scores.
During the scoring process, we observed that existing automated text evaluation methods tend to significantly favor longer responses, as they often contain more information.
However, in the context of speech, overly long responses can burden the user.
Therefore, we designed our evaluation prompts to avoid such a bias towards length (see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Evaluation Prompt ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further enhance the accuracy of evaluation, we devised a query-aware evaluation method for suitable queries.
Specifically, we crafted comprehensive and customized checklists for open-ended queries.
This checklist serves as a structured guide, enabling more precise assessment of the model’s output, thereby contributing to a more robust and reliable evaluation of the end-to-end speech LLMs within our dataset.
Besides assessing the linguistic and semantic qualities of the model’s speech output, we also evaluated its audio acoustic quality using UTMOS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Data Statistics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The final dataset comprises 1,100 queries, with 1,000 drawn from general speech interaction categories (Information Inquiry, Solution Request, Opinion Exchange, Text Creation) and 100 from the Paralinguistic-Featured category. To ensure a comprehensive evaluation of speech models, we carefully curated the dataset to achieve both a balanced distribution across these categories and a diverse thematic spread within each category. This dual-axis balance allows for a thorough assessment of various model capabilities.
The proportion of queries in each category is depicted in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1.2 Speech Generation ‣ 3.1 Dataset Construction ‣ 3 WildSpeech-Bench ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We select state-of-the-art models from several leading companies, including GLM-4-Voice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, MiniCPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, Qwen-2.5-omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, and GPT-4o-Audio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, and evaluate them using WildSpeech-Bench. This section comprehensively evaluates these models’ performance on our benchmark, providing insights into their capabilities and limitations in real-world speech-related tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also introduce a naive pipeline baseline in which Whisper-large-v3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> is used for ASR, Qwen2.5 7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> serves as the LLM, and Melotts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> is used for TTS. However, since our benchmark specifically includes the paralinguistic featured (PF) sub-category, where some crucial paralinguistic information is lost during the ASR process, we expect that this baseline may not be able to effectively handle certain tasks that require understanding of paralinguistic features present in our benchmark.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
