</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Settings ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes the evaluation results of WildSpeech. As shown in the table, GPT-4o-Audio outperforms other models in all categories. Notably, GPT-4o-Audio demonstrates not only superior performance in general conversational tasks, but also maintains a significant lead in paralinguistic-featured queries, further highlighting its comprehensive capabilities. Among all open-source models evaluated, Qwen-2.5-omni achieves the highest overall performance, underscoring the effectiveness of its integrated thinker-talker architecture and the advantages of jointly modeling both reasoning and speech generation within a unified framework. However, while Qwen-2.5-omni excels in general dialogue, its performance on paralinguistic-featured queries lags behind that of GLM-4-voice and MiniCPM, indicating that there is still room for improvement in its speech understanding abilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, with a total score of 10 points, even the best model, GPT-4o-Audio, has an average score of only 6.29 points. This indicates that there is still significant room for improvement in current end-to-end speech large models for real-world applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The naive pipeline approach achieves performance comparable to that of GLM-4-Voice and MiniCPM, which is consistent with the findings reported in VoiceBench; nevertheless, it is important to note that current end-to-end speech models still face substantial challenges in both speech understanding and knowledge reasoning. However, we observe that the pipeline method performs significantly worse only on the stress and tone subsets of our PF sub-category. This degradation can be attributed to the loss of critical paralinguistic information during the ASR stage. These results further demonstrate the comprehensiveness and validity of our benchmark, as it effectively highlights the limitations of text-based pipeline systems in handling tasks that require nuanced paralinguistic understanding.</p>
</div>
