<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Acoustic qualities in different tasks.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Noise Impact</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.3 Consistency with Human Judgment ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates the results of the noise addition experiment. The experimental findings indicate that both natural and human voice noises cause a significant decline in the performance of existing models, with human voice noise having a particularly pronounced impact.While current models may achieve strong results in clean speech environments, our evaluation demonstrates that such performance does not necessarily transfer to real-world, noisy scenarios. This discrepancy highlights a critical gap between laboratory benchmarks and practical deployment conditions.
Our results reveal that current models struggle in real-world, noisy environments, underscoring the importance of evaluating models under more realistic acoustic conditions. This finding exposes areas for improvement, such as enhancing the models’ ability to handle background noise and acoustic variations, which can guide future research towards more robust end-to-end speech models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Audio Quality</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.3 Consistency with Human Judgment ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the evaluation of the speech quality generated by different models. We observe significant differences in the acoustic quality of the outputs generated by different models. Specifically, GPT-4o and Qwen2.5-mini achieve the best performance, surpassing GLM-4-Voice, highlighting the performance gap among the models. In addition, the acoustic quality of each model exhibits minimal variation across different categories, which is much smaller than the variation observed in content quality. This is expected because acoustic characteristics may not vary substantially across categories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Case Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We examine two illustrative cases to show several representative challenges faced by end-to-end speech LLMs.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.6 Case Study ‣ 4 Experimental Results ‣ WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking Audio LLMs in Natural Speech Conversation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, the left-side case exemplifies a common hallucination issue. Given an input referring to the album My Arms, Your Hearse, the model erroneously interpreted it as My Arms Are Your Grave. This hallucinatory output led to a low evaluation score, highlighting the model’s vulnerability to semantic misinterpretations.
The right case is a paralinguistic-featured utterance, where two sentences with identical text convey distinct meanings due to variations in prosodic pauses during human-recorded audio. The first sentence denotes that the musician is performing on stage for the fans, while the second suggests that the performance is specifically for the fans located on the stage. Notably, current models struggle to distinguish between such nuanced interpretations, indicating a limitation in handling paralinguistic cues.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21875v1/case_study.svg" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:443/222;" width="443" height="222"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the model response and evaluation results of two cases.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we addressed the pressing need for comprehensive and specialized benchmarks for end-to-end speech LLM evaluation.
By systematically curating realistic and diverse data, introducing controlled variations in speaker and acoustic conditions, and augmenting the dataset with speech-specific challenges, we constructed a benchmark that accurately reflects the characteristics of real-world speech interactions.
Furthermore, our development of customized evaluation criteria enables a more nuanced and reliable assessment of model performance.
We believe that our approach not only facilitates a deeper understanding of the strengths and weaknesses of current speech LLMs, but also provides valuable guidance for future research and development in this rapidly evolving field.</p>
</div>
</section>
